    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "open-voicing",
      "function_role": "primary-solo-guitar-texture",
      "narrative": "Roberts establishes open voicings as the dominant sound of chord melody on guitar \u2014 the \"big sound\" against which close voicings provide contrast (chapter 3, p. 12). Open voicings are produced by moving one or more tones an octave away from a close position: \"Closed voiced chords can be opened up as far as your reach will permit, simply by moving one or more chordal tones up or down an octave\" (chapter 2, p. 6).",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "close-to-open-octave-displacement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "chord-melody",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "chord-melody",
          "citation": "chapter 2 p. 6"
        },
        {
          "source": "chord-melody",
          "citation": "chapter 3 p. 12"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "h",
      "granularity_name": "idiom-application"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "open-voicing",
      "function_role": "note-omission-policy-under-sustain",
      "narrative": "When sustaining an open voicing through a long melody sequence, Roberts prescribes dropping chord tones as fingering demands while relying on listener memory: \"if each chordal tone is sustained for as long as possible, the listener usually remember those notes that have been dropped\" (chapter 2, p. 9). The instruction is to sustain each tone as long as physically possible before releasing \u2014 delay, not avoidance.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "drop-tones-harmonic-memory",
        "sustain-delay-finger-movement"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "chord-melody",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "chord-melody",
          "citation": "chapter 2 p. 9"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "c",
      "granularity_name": "omission-priority"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "open-voicing",
      "function_role": "color-tone-addition-policy",
      "narrative": "Roberts gives explicit permission to add extensions freely to any voicing: \"the addition of the extensions Maj. 7th, 6th, 9th, etc., only affect the color and texture of the chord and do not change its scale function or its role in the progression\" (chapter 4, p. 36). The arranger can reach for richer open voicings without disrupting harmonic logic.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "extensions-as-color-only"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "chord-melody",
      "references": [
