    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "chromatic-passing-chord-altered-shape",
      "narrative": "Roberts prescribes altered minor-seventh chords with raised fifths (m7+5, equivalent to min7b5's enharmonic neighbor) as chromatic passing shapes between diatonic progression points: \"the passing chords G#m7+5, Am7+5\" connect the primary diatonic chords in Example 35 (chapter 3, p. 11). These altered passing shapes serve as connective tissue even when momentarily dissonant with the underlying harmony.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
